# Code Review

When asked to review code, analyze ALL the following areas on every modified file.
Provide the result grouped by area using this format:

- **✅ Passed** — compliant
- **⚠️ Warning** — suggestion, non-blocking
- **🔴 Violation** — must fix before push

---

## 1. Architecture & Layer Separation

- No imports from upper layers (data must not import application, domain must not import application)
- No imports from inner layers going outward (domain must not import data)
- Only elements in shared folder can be cross layers
- Repository interfaces are in `domain/`, implementations in `data/`
- Data interfaces are in `data/`, implementations in `data/`
- ViewModel communicates only with domain layer (use cases/services)
- UI layer (pages/widgets) does not contain business logic
- Feature folders are self-contained: no cross-feature imports without going through `core/`

## 2. Dart Code Quality

- No `dynamic` types unless strictly necessary — prefer strong typing
- No `as` casting without prior `is` check
- No unused imports, variables or parameters
- No deprecated API usage
- `const` constructors used wherever possible
- `final` used for variables that are never reassigned
- `late` used only when initialization is guaranteed before access
- No magic numbers or hardcoded strings — use constants
- Prefer `sealed class` or `enum` over raw strings for state/type representation
- No `print()` in production code — use a logging system

## 3. Null Safety

- No unnecessary `!` (bang operator) — handle null cases explicitly
- No `?.` chains longer than 2 levels — extract into variables
- Default values provided where `null` is not a valid state
- Nullable return types are intentional and documented

## 4. Flutter Widget Quality

- `StatelessWidget` preferred over `StatefulWidget` when no local state is needed
- `build()` method does not exceed ~30 lines
- No business logic inside `build()`, `initState()`, or `dispose()`
- `const` keyword used on widget constructors and static child trees
- `Key` parameter included in widgets used inside lists or conditional rendering
- No inline anonymous widget classes — extract named widgets
- `@immutable` every where is possible for class

## 5. State Management (Riverpod)

- One provider per file
- Providers are properly scoped (feature-level vs global)
- `AsyncValue` used for async states (loading/error/data)
- No `ref.read` inside `build()` — use `ref.watch` for reactive UI
- `ref.read` used only inside callbacks and event handlers
- `autoDispose` used where the provider should not outlive the consumer
- No direct state mutation — always go through Notifier methods

## 6. Error Handling

- Every async call wrapped in `try/catch` with specific exception types
- No empty `catch` blocks — at minimum log the error
- Custom exceptions defined for domain-specific errors
- User-facing errors are handled with proper UI feedback (dialog, snackbar)
- No `catchError` — prefer `try/catch` for readability

## 7. Performance

- No unnecessary `setState()` or provider rebuilds
- Images use proper caching (`CachedNetworkImage` or similar)
- Lists use `ListView.builder` instead of `ListView(children: [...])` for long lists
- No rebuilds of entire widget trees — use `const`, `select`, or granular providers
- Animations use `AnimatedBuilder` or `AnimatedWidget` instead of rebuilding parent
- No repeated `MediaQuery.of(context)` calls — cache in a local variable
- Dispose controllers, streams, and subscriptions in `dispose()`

## 8. Security

- No credentials, API keys, or secrets in the codebase
- No sensitive data logged or printed
- User input validated and sanitized before processing
- No data persisted in plain text that should be encrypted

## 9. Naming & Conventions

## Coding Conventions

- Code Language: english
- Comments language: english
- File naming: `snake_case`
- Class naming: `PascalCase`
- Variable and method naming: `camelCase`
- Private members prefixed with `_`
- Boolean variables/methods use `is`, `has`, `should`, `can` prefix
- Page files: `<feature>_page.dart`
- Widget files: `<name>_widget.dart`
- DTO files: `<name>_dto.dart`
- Entity files: `<name>_entity.dart`
- Every provider in his riverpod file
- Use Flutter and dart formatter
- No `dynamic` types unless strictly necessary — prefer strong typing
- No `as` casting without prior `is` check
- No unused imports, variables or parameters
- No deprecated API usage
- `const` constructors used wherever possible
- `final` used for variables that are never reassigned
- `late` used only when initialization is guaranteed before access
- No hardcoded or magic value, insert in the specific dart file contained in the *constants* folder.
- Use `@immutable` every where is possible for class
- No `catchError` — prefer `try/catch` for readability
- No unnecessary `!` (bang operator) — handle null cases explicitly
- No `?.` chains longer than 2 levels — extract into variables
- Default values provided where `null` is not a valid state
- Nullable return types are intentional and documented
- Max line length: 120 characters

## 10. Platform & Responsiveness

- No platform-specific code without `Platform` check or conditional import
- UI adapts to different screen sizes (no fixed pixel widths on containers)
- Safe areas handled (`SafeArea`, `MediaQuery.padding`)
- Keyboard overflow handled on form pages (`SingleChildScrollView` or `resizeToAvoidBottomInset`)
- Text scales properly with system font size settings (`MediaQuery.textScaleFactor`)

## 11. Dependencies & Imports

- No unused dependencies in `pubspec.yaml`
- Import order: dart → flutter → packages → project (relative)
- Prefer relative imports within the same feature
- No circular dependencies between features
- New packages justified and reviewed for maintenance/popularity

